        "## Questions\n",
        "\n",
        "1. What is the purpose and benefits of data augmentation?\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "fAaeTijfaEX0"
      },
      "source": [
        "Data augmentation is used to create more variety in the training data by slightly changing existing samples, like rotating or shifting images. The idea is to expose the model to more variations so it doesn’t just memorize the training set. This helps reduce overfitting and makes the model more reliable when it sees new or noisy data. For CAPTCHAs, this is useful because real inputs can look slightly distorted or shifted.\n",
        "\n",
        "```\n",
        "# This is formatted as code\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "VllqyrFaaEX0"
      },
      "source": [
        "2. What is the purpose of 1\\*1 convolution kernel?\n",
        "   "
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "KqaFqS3CaEX0"
      },
      "source": [
        "A 1×1 convolution mainly helps combine and reduce feature channels without changing the image size. It’s often used to shrink the number of channels, which makes the network faster and more efficient. Even though the filter is small, it lets the model mix information from different features and learn better combinations before the next layer.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "VuwWA4pFaEX0"
      },
      "source": [
        "3. What is the purpose of dropout? Does it work on improving the convergence of training or on generalization? Give some reasons to support your idea."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Dropout randomly turns off some neurons during training so the network can’t rely too much on any single feature. This pushes the model to learn more stable and general patterns instead of memorizing the data. Dropout mostly helps with generalization, not convergence — training can actually be a bit slower because of the randomness, but the model usually performs better on unseen data.\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "SeL-uEBL_SSG"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "B5FrTTs5aEX0"
      },
      "source": [
        "Dropout randomly turns off some neurons during training so the network can’t rely too much on any single feature. This pushes the model to learn more stable and general patterns instead of memorizing the data. Dropout mostly helps with generalization, not convergence — training can actually be a bit slower because of the randomness, but the model usually performs better on unseen data."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "LPHStUbIaEX0"
      },
      "source": [
        "4. What is the purpose of batch normalization? Does it work on improving the convergence of training or on generalization? Give some reasons to support your idea."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Xr0HbypbaEX0"
      },
      "source": [
        "Batch normalization keeps activations at a more consistent scale by normalizing them during training. This makes the optimization process smoother and usually helps the model train faster and more reliably. It mainly improves convergence since gradients become more stable, but it can also help generalization a little because the mini-batch statistics add some noise during training."
      ]
    },
